Because the possibility of explicit unanimous consent seems to be the source of uneasiness with the Weberian conception in such cases, it's natural to seek a modified conception of government, and thus anarchy, that considers not only whether a governance authority or arrangement has a territorial monopoly, but also whether the persons it governs have unanimously and voluntarily consented to be governed by it.
 Under this conception, coercion at the level of whether or not you will be bound by a governing authority's decisions, in addition to a territorial monopoly, is what makes for a government.
